# Compact coding style

Prefer single-line statements over multi-line splits for short calls, arrays of few keys, ternaries, and HTML tags with several attributes.

## Do

```php
<?php the_post_thumbnail('full', ['class' => 'w-full h-auto object-cover rounded-3xl', 'alt' => esc_attr(get_the_title())]); ?>
<?php echo $title ? esc_html($title) : __('Fallback', 'novavilla'); ?>
$acfGroup->basicFields->addText('field', 'Label', ['default_value' => $x, 'width' => '40']);
```

```html
<swiper-container class="w-full" slides-per-view="1" space-between="12" loop="true" pagination="false" navigation="true" navigation-next-el="#next" navigation-prev-el="#prev">
<a href="<?php echo esc_url($btn['url']); ?>" <?php echo !empty($btn['target']) ? 'target="' . esc_attr($btn['target']) . '"' : ''; ?> class="primary-button">
```

## Don't

- Split short attribute lists across lines
- Break ternaries across lines when they fit on one line
- Break ACF helper args or small associative arrays across lines when they have only a few keys

Long content strings or large data arrays may stay multi-line when readability clearly requires it.
